  "description": "would have been the absolute\ncutting edge of mountain bike technology. It would have sold for more\nthan $1200. Its frame geometry would have been hailed as a radical departure\nfrom the long chainstays and slack angles of the slow-handling, poor-climbing\npaperboy bike clones of the day. The seven-speed Shimano MegaRange gearing\nwould have been two more gears than the early 80s standard. The Boulevard's\nweight would have undercut its competitors by a solid two pounds, with\nat least 400 grams of the difference in tire weight alone. Front wheels\nwere held on by axle nuts, not quick releases. Compared to the stopping\npower of the brakes back then, the Boulevard's would have been total eye-bulgers.\nBelieve me. I was there.\n\nNow it's nearly two decades later and the Boulevard is just another pretty\nface in the crowd. Timing is everything, I guess. Thanks to the miracle\nof mass-production, all those fine attributes I mentioned are still there,\nbut now you get them for a fraction of what you would have paid back then.\n\nThe Boulevard is good bike made from real stuff that will last you a long\ntime. If you can't have fun riding this bike, don't come whining to me,\nkiddo. Why, back in my day…",
